/// The chrome rule that separates application chrome from its content.
/// The frozen frames carry `#3a3a3a` for the rule between the status strip
/// and the page body.

//! Chosen-item ink under a modal backdrop: controllable, default unchanged.
//!
//! Frozen evidence: `crates/termrock-controls/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` pins
//! checked marks (`Part::MARKER`, `CHECKED`) keeping the accent while focus
//! is elsewhere and under a modal backdrop (owner decision 2026-10-17,
//! `InactiveSelection::Accent`), while `Theme::chosen_in(Accent, ..)` dropped
//! to primary ink under a backdrop — the delta that kept the recipes in place
//! (migration matrix, 2026-10-21). [`BackdropSelection`] makes the
//! under-backdrop behavior explicit; the reference default stays `Neutral`
//! and is not flipped.

//! Port of the PR #26 reference backdrop tests (`src/theme.rs`
//! f4867e9b5f8a08a3bb61ce80d644198a29f51984, `backdrop` → parity
//! `Theme::backdrop_style`), including the per-tier checks that pin the
//! modal-backdrop mapping at every color level.

//! Production-source audit for Escape hints.
//!
//! The frozen reference has one intentional exception: Jackin's cancel
//! action is labelled `Keep launching`. The baseline Dialog contract tests
//! that exact visible label at `src/widgets/dialog.rs:1987-1997` in source
//! commit `4169783ae883788186d161af09ba9c481bd84e5c`. The current Jackin
//! presentation source carries two matching Escape hints. Keep this exception
//! tied to that source file and label; every other non-verb label fails.

/// Return whether the cfg(test) module is inline and the `{` or `;` ending
/// its declaration header. Only inspect that header; never search into the
/// next Rust item when an external module ends with `;`.

//! Theme-level port of the PR #26 reference `tests/focus_gutter.rs`
//! (f4867e9b5f8a08a3bb61ce80d644198a29f51984).
//!
//! The reference drives whole widgets (Button, ListBox, TextInput); the parity
//! port pins the shared theme API those widgets must adopt
//! (`gutter_symbol`, `gutter`, `chosen_in`, `inactive_look`, `legible_mark`).
//! Widget-level adoption is tracked as a handoff: controls currently paint
//! gutters through the recipe system (`Part::GUTTER`) and apps hand-paint
//! `▎`.

//! The focus-bar policy table, measured against the frozen widget output.
//!
//! Truth table: `crates/termrock-controls/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` and
//! `crates/termrock-navigation/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` (commit-scoped
//! audit, 2026-10-21). The audit found no single baked-in rule reproducing
//! every family, so the rule is a [`FocusBarPolicy`] parameter:
//!
//! - `BUTTON` draws the bar only while focused AND focus-visible, and a
//!   focus-visible bar survives the disabled state (truecolor/256/16/mono).
//! - `CHOICE`/`TREE`/`STEPS` rows draw the bar while focused even when
//!   disabled at the truecolor, 256 and 16 tiers; the mono tier blanks it.
//! - `LIST` rows blank the bar while disabled at every tier.
//! - No family needs focus visibility for a focused row, and no family draws
//!   a bar without focus.

//! Adoption example: the upgraded helpers reproduce the frozen widget ink.
//!
//! For every frozen paint site the audit measured (migration matrix,
//! 2026-10-21; truth tables in `crates/termrock-controls/tests/
//! frozen_ink_pins.rs` and `crates/termrock-navigation/tests/
//! frozen_ink_pins.rs`), this renders the same gutter cell twice — once
//! through the frozen recipe path (`Theme::resolve`) and once through the
//! upgraded helpers (`Theme::focus_bar_symbol` + `Theme::gutter_in` with
//! `GutterCell::Inherit`) — into a one-cell buffer and asserts the cells
//! equal. Marker ink goes through `Theme::chosen_in_with` with
//! `BackdropSelection::Accent`. A controls/navigation paint site can now
//! adopt the helpers byte-identically at these states.

//! Gutter-cell fill policy: inherit the container or stamp an explicit blank.
//!
//! Frozen evidence: `crates/termrock-controls/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` and
//! `crates/termrock-navigation/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` pin the bar cell's
//! `bg == None` (the container background shows through) and the audit notes
//! the helpers' explicit `fg=bg` stamp as the byte-difference that blocked
//! adoption (matrix, 2026-10-21). [`GutterCell::Inherit`] is the frozen path;
//! [`GutterCell::Fill`] reproduces the reference behaviour.

//! Executable consumer exercising the shell crate through the public
//! Termrock APIs only: the [`termrock_shell::AppShell`] frame, a
//! [`termrock_shell::Router`] of pages, and the [`termrock_shell::KeyRouter`]
//! key tiers, with unrelated domain data (a file-browser trail).

//! Chrome geometry: how the shell splits its frame into header, body and
//! footer regions.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/chrome.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The source's
//! `Menus<C>` command map and `Chrome::fit` dialog inset are not ported: the
//! parity menu bar resolves choices through [`termrock_core::action::ActionKey`]
//! itself, and the parity dialog owns its chrome through its own layout, so
//! both belong to their component crates rather than to the shell.

//! The footer's ownership rules: which hints it names, in what order, and
//! what the status line does to them.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/footer.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation keeps the compose rules and drops the rendering: the parity
//! hint bar is `termrock_feedback::HintBar`, which renders a runtime
//! [`termrock_runtime::keymap::HintLayer`]; an app maps the composed
//! [`Hint`]s onto that layer at adoption time. The source's `Kind` status
//! variant and the peek (`ui::reveal`) integration are not ported: parity
//! status tone is a theme [`Role`] and the reveal machinery belongs to the
//! runtime's feedback wave.

//! Page routing: the navigation stack, tabs and page identity.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/nav.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation returns [`Option`] where the source asserted internal
//! invariants, and the breadcrumb is the shell-owned [`Crumb`] model because
//! the parity breadcrumb widget is owned by another crate wave.

//! The quit guard: what the app stands to lose when it leaves, and the
//! confirmation that protects it.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/ui/leave.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation describes the confirmation as a [`LeaveDialog`] (kind, title,
//! body and the resolved meaning of each action) instead of building the
//! source's `Dialog` widget directly, because the parity dialog is owned by
//! the overlays crate and resolves through `ActionKey`s; the app maps the
//! guard's [`LeaveDialogKind`] onto whatever dialog component it renders.

//! The reachability harness: does every word a screen shows at a generous
//! size stay reachable at every shipping size?
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/reach.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation drives the [`termrock_runtime::Runtime`] directly over a test
//! buffer instead of the source's `Application` trait, and wheels through
//! [`termrock_core::event::MouseKind::Wheel`] (axis plus notch delta) instead
//! of four wheel kinds. The source's `Words::add_overflowed` and the
//! reveal-driven `Reveal` steps are not ported: they read the source's
//! `ui::reveal` overflow log, which the parity feedback wave owns; a case
//! that hides words only behind truncation reveals stays an allow-file row
//! until that wave lands.

/// Check the found reach against the allow file. Every scenario line reads
/// `<app> <scenario> <WxH> <word...> # <reason>`; a fixed row must drop its
/// line and a new row must carry a reason.

//! A plain text report: a heading, an optional subheading, an optional
//! empty-state line and a list of body lines.
//!
//! The block owns the row grid the report paints on and the columns it
//! insets its lines by. The app supplies the words and the policy; nothing
//! here knows about the domain the report describes.
//!
//! It is the multi-line sibling of `termrock_feedback::TextSection`: a
//! section paints a single body line, a report paints a list and an empty
//! state.

//! The shell's Esc vocabulary (ported from PR #26 `tests/esc_vocabulary.rs`,
//! commit `f4867e9b5`, scoped to the shell crate): Esc never quits, and the
//! notes the shell writes come from one standard phrase set.
//!
//! The source's test scanned every app's rendered hints; the shell crate owns
//! only its own vocabulary, so the floor here covers the shell's defaults and
//! the quit keys that answer Esc.

